course: everyday
lesson: 260
title: Learning a skill, and coins and notes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Skills grow with practice: ten minutes every day builds a skill much faster than one long session a week, and reviewing something a few days later helps the memory last.
explain: Everybody learns at a different speed, so you compare yourself with how you were last month, not with a faster learner, and you give a new club a fair try for several weeks, because the start is the hardest part.
explain: "Some people are just bad at maths" is a myth: maths grows with practice like any other skill, and believing you can improve helps you keep trying.
explain: A coin's value is the number on it, not its size: in the USA a dime (10 cents) is smaller than a nickel (5 cents), and in Australia the $2 coin is smaller than the $1 coin.
explain: Tapping a card or a phone spends real money from a bank account; a girl in Toronto who buys game coins on her mum's phone is spending her mum's real dollars.
fact: Practising a little every day, even ten minutes, builds a skill faster than one long session a week.
fact: Everybody learns at a different speed, so you compare yourself with how you were last month.
fact: You give a new club or lesson a fair try for several weeks, then decide with a parent.
fact: Practical skills, such as tying shoelaces, are learned by practising slowly while an adult shows you step by step.
fact: Touch typing, with all fingers starting on the home row (the middle row of letter keys), is much faster in the long run.
fact: You cook with an adult, wash your hands first and take care with knives and hot pans.
fact: Reviewing what you learned a few days later helps the memory last.
fact: Skill in maths grows with practice, like any other skill.
fact: US coins: a penny is 1 cent, a nickel 5 cents, a dime 10 cents and a quarter 25 cents; UK coins go from 1p to £2.
fact: The value of a coin is shown by the number on it, not by its size.
fact: A found wallet or purse is given to a parent, a shop or the police without taking anything.
fact: Tapping a card or phone spends real money from a bank account, and it can run out.
exercise: You are learning the recorder. Which plan works best? | Ten minutes of practice every day | One hour every Sunday | Only before the school concert | Whenever you remember
exercise: Your friend learned to swim faster than you. Who should you compare yourself with? | Yourself last month | The fastest swimmer in the class | An Olympic swimmer on television | Your friend every day
exercise: After one week of judo you want to quit. What is wise? | Try it for several weeks, then decide with a parent | Quit straight away, as the first week shows everything | Never try a new club again | Keep going for years without asking yourself
exercise: How do most children learn to tie their shoelaces? | Practising slowly while an adult shows them | Reading a long book about knots and how sailors tie them | Watching once very quickly | Waiting until they are older
exercise: Where do your fingers start when you learn to touch type? | On the home row of the keyboard | On the space bar only, with both thumbs | On the number keys along the top row | Wherever is comfortable each time
exercise: You want to make pancakes. What is the safe way? | Cook with an adult, wash hands, take care with the hot pan | Cook alone while everyone is out of the house | Leave the pan to heat while you play | Hold the hot pan with bare hands
exercise: Last week you learned the 7 times table, but now you are forgetting it. What helps? | Review it briefly every few days | Learn it once more the night before a test | Give up, since you forgot it anyway | Learn the 8 times table instead, it's easier
exercise: Your cousin says, "I'm just bad at maths." What is true? | Maths skill grows with practice, like any skill | Some people can never learn maths, however hard they try | Only adults can get good at maths | Maths ability is fixed at birth
exercise: How many cents is a US quarter worth? | 25 cents | 10 cents | 5 cents | 50 cents
exercise: A US dime is smaller than a nickel. Which is worth more? | The dime, at ten cents | The nickel, because it is bigger | They are worth the same | It depends on the year it was made
exercise: How do you know a coin's value? | Read the number on it | Look at how big it is next to others | Weigh it in your hand to feel it | Look at its colour and shine
exercise: You find a purse with cards and money on the pavement. What do you do? | Give it to a parent, a shop or the police, taking nothing | Keep the money and post the cards back | Leave it where it lies | Take only a little of the money for your trouble, then hand it in
exercise: Your dad pays for the shopping by tapping his phone. What is true? | Real money leaves his bank account | The shopping is free | The phone makes new money | Only coins are real money
exercise: A girl in Sydney has a $1 coin and a $2 coin. The $2 coin is smaller. Which is worth more? | The $2 coin | The $1 coin | They are worth the same | The bigger one, always
test: Two boys in Ohio learn the piano. Max practises ten minutes every day; Sam practises one hour every Saturday. Who probably improves faster? | Max, because regular practice builds skill faster | Sam, because he practises longer at once | Both the same, as the minutes over a week add up to the same | Sam, because Saturdays are less tiring
test: In Leeds, a girl has 10p, 5p and 1p. How much does she have? | 16p | 15p | 11p | 6p
test: A boy in Florida has a quarter, a dime and a nickel. How much money is that? | 40 cents | 35 cents | 30 cents | 45 cents
test: A little boy chooses a nickel instead of a dime because "it is bigger, so it's worth more". What has he got wrong? | Value is shown by the number on the coin, not its size | A nickel is always worth more | Bigger coins are always worth more than smaller ones, everywhere | Coins all have the same value
test: Your sister's online game asks to tap mum's card for 500 gems. Why should she ask first? | It spends mum's real money from her account | Gems are free, so it does not matter | Card money is pretend money | The game pays the money back later
test: After three weeks of guitar, a girl in Arizona can play two songs, though her friend can play ten. How should she see her progress? | She has improved a lot since she started | She is hopeless at guitar and always will be | She should quit because her friend is better | She must catch up with her friend by tomorrow
test: Why is it good to review spelling words again a few days after learning them? | Reviewing at intervals helps memories last | Words change their spelling after a week | It is only to please the teacher | It makes the words harder to forget for others
test: A boy in Ireland says, "I'll never be good at maths, so why try?" What would help him most? | Knowing that maths grows with practice | Avoiding all maths lessons from now on | Copying his friend's homework | Only doing the easy sums so he never gets anything wrong
test: You find a wallet with a driving licence and $50 in a park in Seattle. Why give it all back? | The owner needs their cards and money | The police will pay you double for handing it in | The wallet is worth more than the money | Wallets cannot be kept for more than a day
test: got 2 quarters n a dime, no wait 3 quarters n a dime. how much is that | 85 cents | 60 cents | 35 cents | 4 cents
test: my bro says the big coin's always worth more. true? | No, the number on the coin shows its value | Yes, bigger coins are worth more | Yes, heavier coins always mean more money | Only for silver coins
test: piano club is so hard, been 1 week, wanna quit | Give it a fair try for some weeks, then decide with a parent | Quit today, it won't get any easier than it is now | Never quit anything, ever | Quit and start three new clubs
test: just tap mum's phone to pay, it's not real money anyway | It is real money from her bank account | It's free, phones make the money themselves | It's pretend money, like a game | Only cash is real money
explain: A toddler in Florida found a coin on the floor and swallowed it; it stuck in his throat and he needed a doctor to take it out.
explain: A boy in New Zealand called Tom kept a pile of cash in his schoolbag to show off; it was stolen at break, and he lost his birthday money.
fact: Coins are one of the things small children swallow most often, and a stuck coin can block breathing.
fact: Showing off money makes it more likely to be stolen.
test: lil bro has coins in his mouth | Take them out gently and tell an adult | Let him, coins are clean | Give him more to play with | Wait until he spits them out
test: Why did Tom lose his birthday money? | He showed it off and it was stolen | Money disappears by itself after a while | The bank took it | He spent it on lunch
test: wanna bring all my savings to school to show everyone | Leave it at home, it could be lost or stolen | Bring it, it's safe zipped in your bag | Bring it, nobody steals at school | Bring it but only show it to your closest friends at break
test: did u hear a toddler swallowed a coin and it got stuck and he couldn't breathe? real lesson? | Keep coins away from small children; they choke | Coins always pass through safely | Only big coins are dangerous | Toddlers can swallow small coins safely
test: apparently a kid flashed his birthday money at the bus stop and some older kids took it all. what's the true lesson? | Don't show off money; it invites theft | Carry more money so you can share it | Only show it to older kids | Keep it in your hand, then nobody can take it from you
test: my mate says a dime is worth less than a nickel cos it's smaller. right? | No, a dime is 10 cents and a nickel 5 | Yes, smaller coins are worth less | Yes, nickels are worth more because they're bigger | No, they're worth the same
exam: Identify the value of a US quarter. | Twenty-five cents | Ten cents | Fifty cents | Five cents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the value of a coin? | It is shown by the number upon it, not by its size | Larger coins are invariably of greater value | Heavier coins are of greater value | Older coins are of greater value than newly minted coins of the same kind
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon finding a wallet or purse. | It is given to a parent, a shop or the police, untouched | The money is kept and the wallet discarded | It is kept if nobody saw it found | It is left in the street, where its owner is likely to return for it
exam: Ascertain the manner of practice that builds a skill most quickly. | A little every day, even ten minutes | One long session each week | Only on holidays | Only when a test or performance approaches
